LIB_LOAD("gm",{"gm1":{"challenge":[{"q":"In which year was George Müller born?","o":["1805","1905","1705","1848"],"why":"In Prussia."},{"q":"What was George's father's job?","o":["Tax collector","Baker","Soldier","Pastor"],"why":"George stole the money he collected."},{"q":"How old was George when his mother died?","o":["Fourteen","Four","Forty","Twenty"],"why":"He was out drinking that night."},{"q":"Why was George put in jail?","o":["For not paying hotel bills","For fighting","For stealing a horse","For singing loudly"],"why":"At sixteen."},{"q":"Where did George go to university?","o":["Halle","Oxford","Lagos","Paris"],"why":"To study divinity."},{"q":"When was the Saturday night prayer meeting?","o":["November 1825","January 1900","June 1805","March 1898"],"why":"When George was twenty."},{"q":"Who knelt and prayed at the meeting?","o":["Mr Kayser","Beta","George's father","The jailer"],"why":"George had never seen that before."},{"q":"What did George call that night?","o":["The turning point of his life","A boring evening","A mistake","A party"],"why":"Everything changed."},{"q":"Why was George's father angry?","o":["George wanted to be a poor missionary","George stopped studying","George got married","George moved to England"],"why":"No more pennies from him."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Draw nigh to God, and he will draw nigh to ___.\"","o":["you","them","heaven","nobody"],"why":"James 4:8."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What did George hide stolen money in as a boy?","o":["His shoe","A tree","The church","A book"],"why":"But he couldn't hide from God."},"choices":[{"title":"Easy money","q":"You find your mum's purse open on the table with lots of cash. Nobody is around. You want new earphones.","o":[{"t":"Close the purse and leave it. Ask her about earning money for the earphones.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Young George stole from his father. Honesty is the better road."},{"t":"Take a little. She won't miss it.","f":"That's exactly how George started."},{"t":"Take it, and if she asks, blame your sibling.","f":"Now it's stealing and lying."}]},{"title":"The invitation","q":"There's a classmate who seems lost: always in trouble, always angry. You go to a youth fellowship on Fridays.","o":[{"t":"Invite them kindly: \"Want to come with me on Friday?\"","best":true,"f":"Best choice! One invitation from Beta changed George's whole life."},{"t":"Don't bother. They'd never come.","f":"George was 'never going to come' either."},{"t":"Tell them they're going to hell.","f":"An invitation works better than a threat."}]},{"title":"Promises to change","q":"You keep promising yourself you'll stop staying up late on your phone, and you keep failing.","o":[{"t":"Pray about it honestly, ask an adult to help, and make a practical plan (like charging it outside your room).","best":true,"f":"Best choice! George learned that promises alone aren't enough. You need God's help, and other people's."},{"t":"Give up. You'll never change.","f":"If God changed George, he can help you."},{"t":"Promise even harder tomorrow.","f":"Willpower alone usually fails."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"prussia","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 1 of George Müller's story in real life."},{"sc":"prussia","n":"🪞 Be honest about your past. God's grace is for real sinners."},{"sc":"prison","n":"🔒 Promises can't change a heart. Ask God to do it."},{"sc":"parlour","n":"✉️ Invite someone. One invitation can change a life."},{"sc":"study","n":"🌱 Nobody is too far gone. God makes people new."}],"stops":{"gm1s1":{"story":[{"sc":"prussia","n":"George Müller was born in 1805 in {Prussia}, in a part of Europe that is now Germany. His father collected taxes for the government."},{"sc":"prussia","n":"George became famous for trusting God for everything. But that's not how his story starts. In his own book, George was honest about how bad he was as a boy."},{"sc":"prussia","n":"Before he was ten years old, he was stealing money that his father had collected for the government. His father noticed and punished him, but George just got sneakier."},{"sc":"prussia","w":"muellerboy","e":"smirk","t":"If I hide it in my shoe, he'll never find it."},{"sc":"prussia","n":"He lied to his parents all the time. He spent money on sweets, games and, as he got older, drinking."},{"sc":"prussia","n":"When he was fourteen, his mother died. On the night she died, George was out late, playing cards and drinking with friends. He didn't even know she was gone until the next day."},{"sc":"prussia","n":"Even that didn't change him. George admits that he got worse, not better."},{"sc":"prussia","n":"Why would a man write such terrible things about himself? Because he wanted everyone to see how great God's mercy was. The worse he had been, the more amazing God's grace looked."}],"quote":{"text":"This is a faithful saying, and worthy of all acceptation, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief.","who":"1 Timothy 1:15","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Honest about the past","text":"George didn't hide his past to look good. He told the truth about how badly he had lived, so people would see how great God's mercy is. You don't have to pretend to be perfect. God's grace is for real sinners."},"think":"Why do you think George wrote honestly about his bad past instead of hiding it?","quiz":[{"q":"Where was George Müller born?","o":["Prussia (now Germany)","England","Nigeria","Scotland"],"why":"In 1805."},{"q":"What did George do as a young boy?","o":["Stole money and lied","Prayed every day","Helped orphans","Studied hard"],"why":"He was honest about it later."},{"q":"Where was George the night his mother died?","o":["Out playing cards and drinking","At her bedside","At church","At school"],"why":"He didn't know until the next day."},{"q":"Why did George write about his bad past?","o":["To show how great God's mercy is","To brag","To make money","By mistake"],"why":"The worse he was, the greater grace looked."},{"q":"What does 1 Timothy 1:15 say Jesus came to do?","o":["Save sinners","Punish sinners","Ignore sinners","Reward good people only"],"why":"Of whom I am chief."}]},"gm1s2":{"story":[{"sc":"prussia","n":"At sixteen, George went travelling with money his father had given him for school. He spent it all on fun."},{"sc":"inn","n":"He stayed in expensive hotels, ordered the best food, and then sneaked away without paying the bills."},{"sc":"inn","w":"muellerboy","e":"smirk","t":"Another free night! Who's going to catch me?"},{"sc":"inn","n":"Someone did. At one hotel, he tried to slip out again, and was caught. The police arrested him."},{"sc":"prison","n":"George was put in jail. He was sixteen years old, locked in a cell with a thief, for more than three weeks."},{"sc":"prison","w":"jailer","e":"stern","t":"Your father's been sent for. Until he pays your debts, you stay here."},{"sc":"prison","n":"At last his father came, paid everything, and took him home. He was furious, and George was ashamed. But only for a while."},{"sc":"prison","n":"George promised to change. He even tried hard for a time. But he couldn't change his own heart. Something more than promises was needed."}],"quote":{"text":"The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked: who can know it?","who":"Jeremiah 17:9","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Promises aren't enough","text":"In jail, George promised to change, and tried. But on his own, he couldn't. Willpower can change behaviour for a while, but only God can change a heart."},"think":"Have you ever promised yourself you'd stop a bad habit, then failed? Why is real change so hard on our own?","quiz":[{"q":"What did George do with his school money?","o":["Spent it all on fun","Saved it","Gave it to the poor","Bought books"],"why":"Hotels and food."},{"q":"What trick did George play on hotels?","o":["Sneaking away without paying","Cleaning rooms","Singing for food","Nothing"],"why":"Until he was caught."},{"q":"How old was George when he went to jail?","o":["Sixteen","Ten","Thirty","Twenty-five"],"why":"For more than three weeks."},{"q":"Who paid George's debts?","o":["His father","His friend","The jailer","Nobody"],"why":"He was furious."},{"q":"Why didn't George's promises to change work?","o":["He couldn't change his own heart","He didn't make any","He was too young","He forgot them"],"why":"Only God can change a heart."}]},"gm1s3":{"story":[{"sc":"college","n":"George went to the university at {Halle} to study {divinity}, the study of God, so he could become a minister. Not because he loved God, but because ministers had a comfortable, well-paid life."},{"sc":"college","n":"He was still wild, drinking and wasting money. But one Saturday in November 1825, when George was twenty, his friend Beta said something surprising."},{"sc":"college","w":"beta","e":"smile","t":"George, I go to a little meeting at a man's house on Saturday evenings. They read the Bible and pray. Want to come?"},{"sc":"parlour","n":"George went. He felt awkward and out of place. He even apologised for coming."},{"sc":"parlour","n":"They sang a hymn. Then a man named Mr Kayser knelt down on the floor and prayed out loud, asking God to bless the meeting."},{"sc":"parlour","n":"George had never seen anyone kneel to pray like that. He had never heard anyone talk to God as if God were really there, listening."},{"sc":"parlour","w":"mueller","e":"shock","t":"I could not pray like that, though I'm far more educated than this man. Something is different about these people."},{"sc":"parlour","n":"George went home that night with a strange new happiness. He couldn't explain it. But he wanted to come back. That night, he later wrote, was the turning point of his life."}],"quote":{"text":"Draw nigh to God, and he will draw nigh to you.","who":"James 4:8","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"One invitation","text":"George's life turned around because a friend invited him to a small prayer meeting. Never underestimate a simple invitation. You never know what God might do through it."},"think":"Beta just invited George to a meeting. Who could you invite to church, fellowship or a Bible study?","quiz":[{"q":"Why did George study divinity at first?","o":["Because ministers had a comfortable life","Because he loved God","His mother told him","He liked Latin"],"why":"Not for the right reasons."},{"q":"Who invited George to the prayer meeting?","o":["His friend Beta","His father","The jailer","A teacher"],"why":"A simple invitation."},{"q":"What did Mr Kayser do that amazed George?","o":["Knelt down and prayed out loud","Sang a solo","Preached for hours","Gave him money"],"why":"As if God were really there."},{"q":"How did George feel going home?","o":["A strange new happiness","Bored","Angry","Sleepy"],"why":"The turning point of his life."},{"q":"What does James 4:8 promise?","o":["Draw near to God and he will draw near to you","God is far away","God only comes to good people","Stay away"],"why":"George drew near."}]},"gm1s4":{"story":[{"sc":"study","n":"George kept going back to the meetings. He started reading the Bible for himself. Slowly, he came to trust in Jesus."},{"sc":"study","n":"Everything began to change. He gave up drinking. He stopped lying. He started praying, really praying, on his own."},{"sc":"study","w":"mueller","e":"smile","t":"I used to love the things that were ruining me. Now I love God's word. Only he could do this."},{"sc":"study","n":"Of course, he still made mistakes. But his heart had turned around, and his life followed."},{"sc":"prussia","n":"Soon George wanted to become a missionary. His father was very angry. He had paid for George's education so he'd have a comfortable job, not to go off as a poor missionary."},{"sc":"prussia","w":"muellerfather","e":"frown","t":"If you do this, don't expect another penny from me."},{"sc":"prussia","n":"George decided not to take any more money from his father. He wanted to depend on God alone. It was the first time he trusted God to provide for his needs. It wouldn't be the last."}],"quote":{"text":"Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new.","who":"2 Corinthians 5:17","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Nobody is too far gone","text":"A thief, a liar and a jailbird became a man of prayer. If God could change George Müller, he can change anyone, including you, and including the person you think is hopeless."},"think":"Is there anyone you've thought was 'too far gone' for God? How does George's story change that?","quiz":[{"q":"What did George start doing after the meetings?","o":["Reading the Bible and praying for himself","Drinking more","Stealing more","Nothing"],"why":"His heart turned around."},{"q":"What did George give up?","o":["Drinking and lying","Eating","Studying","Friends"],"why":"His life followed his heart."},{"q":"What did George want to become?","o":["A missionary","A banker","A soldier","A tax collector"],"why":"His father was angry."},{"q":"What did George decide about his father's money?","o":["Not to take any more, and trust God instead","Ask for more","Steal it","Spend it all"],"why":"The first time he trusted God to provide."},{"q":"According to 2 Corinthians 5:17, what is anyone in Christ?","o":["A new creature","The same as before","Perfect at once","Rich"],"why":"Old things are passed away."}]}}},"gm2":{"challenge":[{"q":"Why did George first come to England?","o":["To train as a missionary","To study law","To work in a mill","To escape jail"],"why":"In 1829."},{"q":"What happened to George soon after arriving?","o":["He became very ill","He got rich","He was arrested","He got married"],"why":"He went to Teignmouth to recover."},{"q":"What did the church at Teignmouth ask George to be?","o":["Their pastor","Their treasurer","Their gardener","Their bell-ringer"],"why":"He hadn't planned it."},{"q":"Why didn't George like pew rents?","o":["They made the rich seem more important than the poor","They were too cheap","They were too complicated","He liked them"],"why":"Unfair to the poor."},{"q":"Who did George tell his needs to?","o":["God alone","Everybody","The newspaper","His father"],"why":"Not even the church."},{"q":"What did George write down?","o":["Every need, prayer and answer","Only his sermons","Only his debts","Nothing"],"why":"Proof that God keeps his promises."},{"q":"Whose sister was Mary Groves?","o":["A missionary's (Anthony Norris Groves)","The Queen's","Henry Craik's","Mr Kayser's"],"why":"She came from a family who loved missions."},{"q":"Who moved to Bristol with George?","o":["Henry Craik","Beta","His father","Mr Wagner"],"why":"In 1832."},{"q":"What did George and Henry do during the cholera?","o":["Visited the sick and prayed with them","Ran away","Sold medicine","Closed their doors"],"why":"Like Jesus said."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Seek ye first the kingdom of ___.\"","o":["God","money","Bristol","heaven"],"why":"Matthew 6:33."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What did George put at the back of the chapel instead of taking a salary?","o":["A box for gifts","A bank","A ticket booth","A vending machine"],"why":"He told his needs to God alone."},"choices":[{"title":"Telling God first","q":"You need money for a school trip and you're worried. You're about to complain to everyone about it.","o":[{"t":"Pray about it first, then talk calmly with your parents about options.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! George's secret: tell your needs to God first."},{"t":"Complain loudly so people feel sorry for you.","f":"George told God, not everyone else."},{"t":"Decide God doesn't care about small things.","f":"George wrote down answers to small prayers too."}]},{"title":"Front seat","q":"At a church event, the best seats are reserved for 'important guests'. A poor elderly woman is left standing at the back.","o":[{"t":"Give her your seat and help her get comfortable.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! George hated it when the poor got the worst seats."},{"t":"Ignore it. It's not your event.","f":"Small kindnesses matter to God."},{"t":"Grab the last good seat for yourself.","f":"That's the pew rent attitude."}]},{"title":"Visiting the sick","q":"A friend has been in hospital for two weeks. Visiting feels awkward and you don't know what to say.","o":[{"t":"Go anyway, or send a kind message. Just being there means a lot.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! George went towards the sick, even during cholera."},{"t":"Wait until they're better.","f":"They need you now."},{"t":"Assume someone else has visited.","f":"Be the someone."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"seashore","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 2 of George Müller's story in real life."},{"sc":"church","n":"🧭 Make plans, but let God direct your steps."},{"sc":"home","n":"📦 Tell your needs to God first. Try a prayer journal: write prayers and answers."},{"sc":"home","n":"💍 Find faith partners who pray with you."},{"sc":"sickroom","n":"🏥 Go towards the sick and hurting, not away from them."}],"stops":{"gm2s1":{"story":[{"sc":"seashore","n":"In 1829, George sailed to England to train as a missionary. He could hardly speak English, and he spoke it with a strong German accent all his life."},{"sc":"bristol","n":"Soon after arriving in London, he became very ill. He thought he might die. Friends sent him to the seaside town of {Teignmouth} to recover."},{"sc":"church","n":"There, while he got better, he met Christians who loved the Bible deeply. One of them, Henry Craik, became his closest friend for life."},{"sc":"church","w":"craik","e":"smile","t":"George, your English is terrible, but your heart for God is wonderful! Will you preach for us?"},{"sc":"church","n":"The little church at Teignmouth asked George to be their pastor. He hadn't planned it at all. But he could see that God was leading him."},{"sc":"church","w":"mueller","e":"smile","t":"I planned to go somewhere far away. But God has put me here. I'll stay where he leads."}],"quote":{"text":"A man's heart deviseth his way: but the LORD directeth his steps.","who":"Proverbs 16:9","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"God directs your steps","text":"George had one plan, but illness led him to Teignmouth, a best friend and a church. Make plans, but hold them loosely. God often leads us through surprises, even through illness."},"think":"Has a change of plans ever led you somewhere good? How might God be directing your steps right now?","quiz":[{"q":"When did George come to England?","o":["1829","1805","1898","1850"],"why":"To train as a missionary."},{"q":"How was George's English?","o":["Poor, with a strong German accent","Perfect","He didn't speak it","Better than the English"],"why":"All his life."},{"q":"Why did George go to Teignmouth?","o":["To recover from illness","On holiday","To build a house","To find work"],"why":"He thought he might die."},{"q":"Who became George's closest friend?","o":["Henry Craik","Beta","Mr Kayser","The jailer"],"why":"For life."},{"q":"What does Proverbs 16:9 say?","o":["The LORD directs our steps","Plans always work","Never make plans","Luck decides"],"why":"God leads through surprises."}]},"gm2s2":{"story":[{"sc":"church","n":"At Teignmouth, the church paid George a regular {salary}. Part of it came from {pew rents}: the rich paid for the best seats."},{"sc":"church","w":"richman","e":"smirk","t":"Of course I get the front seat. I pay for it."},{"sc":"church","n":"George didn't like that. Poor people got the worst seats, and it seemed to make the rich more important in church than the poor."},{"sc":"church","n":"And he read Jesus' words: 'Seek first the kingdom of God, and all these things shall be added unto you.' He decided to trust God completely."},{"sc":"church","w":"mueller","e":"smile","t":"From now on, I won't take a fixed salary. We'll put a box at the back of the chapel. Anyone who wants to give can. And I'll tell my needs to God alone."},{"sc":"home","n":"People thought he was foolish. Sometimes George and his family were down to their last coin, with no food for tomorrow."},{"sc":"home","n":"But each time, they prayed. And again and again, money or food arrived just when they needed it, often from people who knew nothing about their need."},{"sc":"home","n":"George wrote all of it down: every need, every prayer, every answer. He wanted proof that God really does keep his promises."}],"quote":{"text":"But seek ye first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you.","who":"Matthew 6:33","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Ask God first","text":"George stopped relying on a salary and told his needs to God alone. You don't have to give up your pocket money! But you can learn his secret: bring every need to God first, and watch how he provides."},"think":"Why do you think George wrote down every prayer and every answer? Could you try keeping a prayer journal?","quiz":[{"q":"What were pew rents?","o":["Money rich people paid for the best church seats","Rent for houses","Bus fares","Taxes"],"why":"George thought it was unfair to the poor."},{"q":"What did George decide about his salary?","o":["Not to take a fixed salary, and trust God","Ask for more","Get a second job","Charge for sermons"],"why":"Seek first the kingdom."},{"q":"What did George put at the back of the chapel?","o":["A box for gifts","A sign asking for money","A collection plate passed round","Nothing"],"why":"He told his needs to God alone."},{"q":"What happened when they ran out of money?","o":["They prayed, and help came just in time","They starved","They borrowed","They stole"],"why":"Again and again."},{"q":"Why did George write everything down?","o":["As proof that God keeps his promises","To remember shopping","To complain","For school"],"why":"Every need, every answer."}]},"gm2s3":{"story":[{"sc":"church","n":"In 1830, George married Mary Groves, a gentle, wise young woman who loved God deeply."},{"sc":"home","w":"marygroves","e":"smile","t":"George, if we're going to trust God for everything, let's do it together."},{"sc":"home","n":"Mary agreed with George's decision about living by faith. They even sold many of their nice things and gave the money to the poor."},{"sc":"home","w":"mueller","e":"smile","t":"We'll have only what we need. Everything else belongs to God's work."},{"sc":"home","n":"It wasn't easy. Some days, they had almost nothing. But they prayed together, trusted together, and laughed together."},{"sc":"home","n":"For forty years, Mary was George's partner in faith and in everything he did. Without her, the great work that was coming would not have happened."}],"quote":{"text":"Two are better than one; because they have a good reward for their labour.","who":"Ecclesiastes 4:9","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Faith partners","text":"George didn't trust God alone. Mary trusted with him. Choose friends, and one day a husband or wife, who will pray with you and encourage your faith. Two are better than one."},"think":"Who is your 'faith partner': someone who prays with you and encourages you to trust God?","quiz":[{"q":"Who did George marry?","o":["Mary Groves","Mary Slessor","Hannah","Lygia"],"why":"In 1830."},{"q":"What did Mary think about living by faith?","o":["She agreed and wanted to do it together","She refused","She laughed","She left"],"why":"Partners in faith."},{"q":"What did they do with many of their nice things?","o":["Sold them and gave to the poor","Kept them","Hid them","Broke them"],"why":"Only what they needed."},{"q":"How long was Mary George's partner?","o":["Forty years","One year","Five years","Ten years"],"why":"In everything he did."},{"q":"What does Ecclesiastes 4:9 say?","o":["Two are better than one","Be alone","Trust nobody","One is enough"],"why":"Faith partners."}]},"gm2s4":{"story":[{"sc":"bristol","n":"In 1832, George and Henry Craik moved to {Bristol}, a busy port city full of ships, merchants, factories and poor families."},{"sc":"bristol","n":"They became pastors of two chapels there. George would live in Bristol for the rest of his long life: more than sixty years."},{"sc":"sickroom","n":"That very summer, a terrible disease called {cholera} swept through Bristol. People died in their hundreds. Church bells rang for funerals almost every day."},{"sc":"sickroom","w":"craik","e":"worried","t":"George, people are dying all over the city. Many are afraid to go near the sick."},{"sc":"sickroom","w":"mueller","e":"stern","t":"Then we will go. They need comfort, and prayer, and the hope of Jesus."},{"sc":"sickroom","n":"George and Henry visited the sick and dying, day after day, praying with them and comforting families."},{"sc":"bristol","n":"Through that hard summer, many people in Bristol came to trust in Jesus. And George began to notice something else in the streets: children with no parents, and nowhere to go."}],"quote":{"text":"I was sick, and ye visited me: I was in prison, and ye came unto me.","who":"Matthew 25:36","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Go towards the hurting","text":"When cholera struck, many hid from the sick. George and Henry went towards them with prayer and comfort. Jesus said that when we visit the sick, it's as if we're visiting him."},"think":"Who is sick, lonely or hurting near you? How could you visit or comfort them this week?","quiz":[{"q":"When did George move to Bristol?","o":["1832","1805","1898","1870"],"why":"With Henry Craik."},{"q":"How long did George live in Bristol?","o":["More than sixty years","One year","Ten years","Never"],"why":"The rest of his long life."},{"q":"What disease struck Bristol in 1832?","o":["Cholera","Malaria","Measles","Flu"],"why":"Spread by dirty water."},{"q":"What did George and Henry do?","o":["Visited the sick and dying","Left the city","Hid at home","Closed the church"],"why":"Go towards the hurting."},{"q":"What did George begin to notice in the streets?","o":["Children with no parents","New shops","Fine horses","Rich visitors"],"why":"His great work was about to begin."}]}}},"gm3":{"challenge":[{"q":"Why were there so many orphans in Bristol?","o":["Many parents died of diseases like cholera","Families moved away","It was a law","Nobody knows"],"why":"Hard times."},{"q":"What did George start for poor children before the orphan house?","o":["Breakfasts and schools","A football club","A bank","A shop"],"why":"Paid for by prayer."},{"q":"What does 'open thy mouth wide' encourage us to do?","o":["Pray big prayers","Eat more","Shout loudly","Sing louder"],"why":"And God will fill it."},{"q":"How much money did George pray for?","o":["A thousand pounds","Ten pounds","A million pounds","Nothing"],"why":"A huge amount then."},{"q":"Who gave nearly all her savings?","o":["A poor seamstress","The Queen","A banker","A sailor"],"why":"A hundred pounds."},{"q":"When did the first orphan house open?","o":["April 1836","December 1835","June 1849","March 1898"],"why":"On Wilson Street."},{"q":"Were the first orphans boys or girls?","o":["Girls","Boys","Both","Babies only"],"why":"The first house was for girls."},{"q":"What did George do when no children came?","o":["Prayed for children","Gave up","Advertised in newspapers","Closed the house"],"why":"He'd forgotten to pray for them."},{"q":"What did George's reports show?","o":["What God had provided, after it was provided","Requests for money","Complaints","Nothing"],"why":"Proof of God's faithfulness."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Without faith it is impossible to ___ him.\"","o":["please","see","find","hear"],"why":"Hebrews 11:6."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What did George forget to pray for when he opened the first orphan house?","o":["The children","The beds","The money","The workers"],"why":"The next day, the children came!"},"choices":[{"title":"A big prayer","q":"Your church is praying for a new building, but you think, \"That's too big. God won't do that.\"","o":[{"t":"Pray boldly and with faith. God can do big things.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! 'Open thy mouth wide, and I will fill it.'"},{"t":"Pray for something smaller, just in case.","f":"George prayed for a thousand pounds when he had nothing."},{"t":"Don't pray. It's the adults' job.","f":"Children's prayers matter to God too."}]},{"title":"Hinting for help","q":"You want your aunt to buy you a new phone. You think about mentioning loudly how old and broken yours is.","o":[{"t":"Tell God about your needs, and if you ask your aunt, ask honestly, not by hinting.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! George refused even to hint. Honesty and trust go together."},{"t":"Drop hints until she feels guilty.","f":"That's manipulating, not trusting."},{"t":"Complain about it constantly.","f":"Complaining isn't the same as praying."}]},{"title":"Forgot to pray","q":"You've planned a birthday party in detail: food, games, decorations. Your mum asks, \"Have you prayed about it?\"","o":[{"t":"Laugh, admit you forgot, and pray about it together.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Even George forgot to pray for the children. In all thy ways acknowledge him."},{"t":"Say God doesn't care about parties.","f":"God cares about every part of your life."},{"t":"Say you'll pray later, and don't.","f":"It only takes a minute."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"bristol","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 3 of George Müller's story in real life."},{"sc":"bristol","n":"👀 Ask God for eyes to see the people others walk past."},{"sc":"study","n":"😮 Pray big prayers. Open your mouth wide!"},{"sc":"home","n":"📋 Pray about everything, even the 'obvious' things."},{"sc":"orphandining","n":"📒 Let your life be living proof that God is real and answers prayer."}],"stops":{"gm3s1":{"story":[{"sc":"bristol","n":"Bristol in the 1830s was rich and poor side by side. And there were many {orphans}: children whose parents had died, often from diseases like cholera."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"Some orphans begged in the streets. Some stole to survive. Many ended up in the {workhouse}, a harsh place where children worked hard for a little food and a bed."},{"sc":"bristol","w":"orphanboy","e":"worried","t":"Spare a penny, sir? I've not eaten since yesterday."},{"sc":"bristol","n":"George couldn't walk past them. He began inviting poor children in for breakfast in the mornings, and teaching them to read the Bible."},{"sc":"bristol","n":"He also started schools for poor children, and gave out Bibles. All of it was paid for by prayer, without asking anyone for money."},{"sc":"bristol","w":"mueller","e":"worried","t":"These children need more than breakfast. They need a home. They need to be loved and raised to know God."},{"sc":"bristol","n":"The idea of an orphan house began to grow in George's heart. But how could a pastor with no salary possibly pay for a whole house full of children?"}],"quote":{"text":"Pure religion and undefiled before God and the Father is this, To visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction.","who":"James 1:27","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Eyes that see","text":"Many people walked past Bristol's orphans every day. George saw them, and couldn't forget them. Ask God to give you eyes to see the people others walk past."},"think":"Who might be the 'unseen' children in your town or school? How could you notice them?","quiz":[{"q":"What are orphans?","o":["Children whose parents have died","Rich children","Children at school","Twins"],"why":"Bristol had many."},{"q":"What was a workhouse?","o":["A harsh place where poor people worked for food and a bed","A factory for toys","A school","A hospital"],"why":"Many orphans ended up there."},{"q":"What did George do for poor children in the mornings?","o":["Gave them breakfast and taught them to read the Bible","Sent them away","Sold them bread","Nothing"],"why":"He couldn't walk past them."},{"q":"What did George think orphans needed most?","o":["A home, love and to know God","Just money","Just bread","Nothing"],"why":"More than breakfast."},{"q":"According to James 1:27, pure religion includes what?","o":["Visiting the fatherless and widows","Being rich","Ignoring the poor","Only going to church"],"why":"Caring for orphans."}]},"gm3s2":{"story":[{"sc":"study","n":"On a December evening in 1835, George was reading his Bible. He came to Psalm 81, verse 10."},{"sc":"study","n":"'Open thy mouth wide, and I will fill it.'"},{"sc":"study","w":"mueller","e":"shock","t":"Open my mouth wide? Then I'll ask God for something big. A house for orphans. Money to run it. And the right people to help."},{"sc":"study","n":"He prayed for a building, for a thousand pounds (a huge amount then), and for workers to care for the children."},{"sc":"church","n":"He held a public meeting to explain his plan. But he refused to take a collection or ask anyone for a single penny."},{"sc":"church","n":"Gifts began to arrive: furniture, cloth, plates, money. Some came from rich people. Many came from poor ones."},{"sc":"church","w":"seamstress","e":"smile","t":"Mr Müller, I sew clothes for a living. Please take this for the children. It's a hundred pounds."},{"sc":"church","n":"A poor seamstress gave a hundred pounds: nearly everything she had saved. George was so surprised that he asked her to think carefully. She insisted. She wanted to give it to God."}],"quote":{"text":"I am the LORD thy God... open thy mouth wide, and I will fill it.","who":"Psalm 81:10","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Pray big","text":"George read 'open thy mouth wide' and prayed boldly for something huge. God loves bold prayers made in faith. Don't only pray small prayers. Ask God for big things that bring him glory."},"think":"What's a 'big prayer' you could pray for your family, school or community?","quiz":[{"q":"Which verse inspired George to start the orphan house?","o":["Psalm 81:10","John 3:16","Genesis 1:1","Psalm 23:1"],"why":"Open thy mouth wide."},{"q":"When did George read it?","o":["December 1835","June 1805","March 1898","May 1950"],"why":"On a December evening."},{"q":"What three things did George pray for?","o":["A building, money and workers","A horse, a cart and a dog","Fame, power and gold","Nothing"],"why":"Something big."},{"q":"What did George refuse to do at his public meeting?","o":["Take a collection or ask for money","Speak","Pray","Explain his plan"],"why":"Only prayer."},{"q":"What did the poor seamstress give?","o":["A hundred pounds, nearly all she had","A penny","A needle","Nothing"],"why":"She wanted to give it to God."}]},"gm3s3":{"story":[{"sc":"bristol","n":"By April 1836, George had a house to rent on {Wilson Street}, furniture, workers and money. The first orphan house was ready!"},{"sc":"bristol","n":"The opening day arrived. The beds were made. The food was ready. The workers waited."},{"sc":"bristol","n":"And nobody came. Not one child applied."},{"sc":"home","w":"mueller","e":"worried","t":"Lord, I prayed for a building, for money, for workers. But I never prayed for children! I just assumed they would come."},{"sc":"home","n":"That evening, George humbly asked God to send the children. He realised he had depended on his own plans in that one area."},{"sc":"bristol","n":"The very next day, the first application arrived. Then more and more. Soon the house was full of girls."},{"sc":"orphandining","w":"orphangirl","e":"big","t":"Is this really my bed? And breakfast every day? Thank you, Mr Müller!"},{"sc":"orphandining","w":"mueller","e":"smile","t":"Don't thank me, child. Thank God. He's the one who provides."}],"quote":{"text":"In all thy ways acknowledge him, and he shall direct thy paths.","who":"Proverbs 3:6","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Pray about everything","text":"George prayed for everything except the most obvious thing: the children! Even great men of prayer forget. Bring every part of your plans to God, even the parts that seem obvious."},"think":"Why do you think George forgot to pray for the children? Are there parts of your life you forget to pray about?","quiz":[{"q":"Where was the first orphan house?","o":["Wilson Street, Bristol","Ashley Down","London","Prussia"],"why":"Opened in April 1836."},{"q":"What happened on opening day?","o":["No children came","Hundreds came","It burned down","Nothing was ready"],"why":"Not one applied."},{"q":"What had George forgotten to pray for?","o":["The children","Money","Furniture","Workers"],"why":"He just assumed they would come."},{"q":"What happened after he prayed?","o":["Applications came the very next day","Nothing","He gave up","He moved house"],"why":"Soon the house was full."},{"q":"Who did George tell the children to thank?","o":["God","Himself","The Queen","The workers"],"why":"He's the one who provides."}]},"gm3s4":{"story":[{"sc":"study","n":"George had three firm rules for the orphan work."},{"sc":"study","n":"Rule one: never ask anyone for money. Not in sermons, not in letters, not even by hinting. Tell needs only to God."},{"sc":"study","n":"Rule two: never go into {debt}. If there was no money for something, they would wait and pray, not borrow."},{"sc":"study","n":"Rule three: publish a report afterwards, showing what God had provided and how. But never report needs while they were still needs."},{"sc":"study","w":"craik","e":"worried","t":"George, why not just tell people when you're short? Many would gladly help."},{"sc":"study","w":"mueller","e":"smile","t":"Because I want the world to see that God is real. If orphans are fed only through prayer, everyone will know that God still hears and answers."},{"sc":"study","n":"That was the heart of it. George wanted to prove to doubting Christians and unbelievers alike that God is faithful still, and hears prayer still."},{"sc":"study","n":"The orphan houses were not only a home for children. They were a living proof of God, in brick and stone."}],"quote":{"text":"That God may be magnified... that God is faithful still, and hears prayer still.","who":"George Müller, on why he began the orphan work","tag":"Müller's words"},"lesson":{"title":"Living proof of God","text":"George's main reason for the orphan houses was to show that God is real and answers prayer. Your life can be living proof of God too, when people see you trust him and see him come through."},"think":"Why do you think George's 'no asking' rule made his work such a powerful testimony?","proverb":{"text":"Ọ̀rọ̀ púpọ̀ kì í kún agbọ̀n.","lang":"Yoruba","mean":"Many words do not fill a basket. Action and faith speak louder than talk."},"quiz":[{"q":"What was rule one?","o":["Never ask anyone for money","Always ask for money","Ask only the rich","Ask once a year"],"why":"Tell needs only to God."},{"q":"What was rule two?","o":["Never go into debt","Borrow when needed","Spend everything","Save nothing"],"why":"Wait and pray instead."},{"q":"When did George report what God had provided?","o":["Afterwards, never while the need was still there","Before every meal","Never","Every hour"],"why":"So nobody would feel pressured."},{"q":"What was George's main reason for the orphan houses?","o":["To show the world that God is real and hears prayer","To become famous","To get rich","To build houses"],"why":"God is faithful still."},{"q":"What does the Yoruba proverb say?","o":["Many words do not fill a basket","Baskets are heavy","Talk a lot","Words are food"],"why":"Faith in action speaks louder."}]}}},"gm4":{"challenge":[{"q":"How often did help arrive 'just in time'?","o":["Again and again","Never","Once","Only at Christmas"],"why":"God was never late."},{"q":"What did George write in his journal?","o":["Every need and every answer","Only bad news","Shopping lists","Nothing"],"why":"Proof of God's faithfulness."},{"q":"In the famous story, who knocked first?","o":["The baker","The milkman","The Queen","A policeman"],"why":"With fresh bread."},{"q":"Why did the milkman give his milk away?","o":["His cart broke down and the milk would go sour","He was told to","He didn't like milk","It was a holiday"],"why":"Right outside the door!"},{"q":"Who complained about the noise on Wilson Street?","o":["The neighbours","The children","The Queen","Henry Craik"],"why":"Politely."},{"q":"Where were the new orphan houses built?","o":["Ashley Down","Wilson Street","London","Teignmouth"],"why":"On a hill with fresh air."},{"q":"How many children could the Ashley Down houses hold at once?","o":["More than two thousand","Fifty","Ten thousand","Three hundred"],"why":"In five huge houses."},{"q":"Do the Ashley Down buildings still exist?","o":["Yes, they still stand","No, they burned down","They were never built","They are underwater"],"why":"More than 170 years later."},{"q":"What did George say was his first business every day?","o":["To get his soul happy in the Lord","To count money","To wake the children","To read the newspaper"],"why":"Since 1841."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Give us this day our daily ___.\"","o":["bread","money","work","news"],"why":"Matthew 6:11."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! At what time did the baker get up to bake bread for the orphans?","o":["Two o'clock in the morning","Noon","Midnight exactly","Six in the evening"],"why":"Before anyone had even prayed."},"choices":[{"title":"Worry about tomorrow","q":"Your family is going through a tight time with money, and you keep lying awake worrying about school fees.","o":[{"t":"Pray about it, thank God for today's provision, and talk to your parents about your worries.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! George trusted God for today's bread, and tomorrow's came tomorrow."},{"t":"Worry harder.","f":"Worry doesn't add a single meal."},{"t":"Keep it all to yourself.","f":"Share your worries with God and your parents."}]},{"title":"First thing in the morning","q":"Every morning you reach for your phone before you're even out of bed.","o":[{"t":"Try reading one Bible verse and praying for a minute before you check your phone.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! George's secret: get your soul happy in the Lord first."},{"t":"Keep scrolling. You'll pray later.","f":"Later often becomes never."},{"t":"Delete the Bible app.","f":"That's the opposite direction!"}]},{"title":"Fair criticism","q":"A neighbour complains that your football keeps landing in their garden and breaking their plants.","o":[{"t":"Listen, apologise, and find a better place to play.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! George listened to his neighbours, and it led to Ashley Down."},{"t":"Argue that they're being grumpy.","f":"Sometimes complaints have a point."},{"t":"Keep kicking it there on purpose.","f":"That's not loving your neighbour."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"orphandining","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 4 of George Müller's story in real life."},{"sc":"home","n":"🍞 Trust God for today. Tomorrow's bread will come tomorrow."},{"sc":"orphandining","n":"🙏 Thank God in advance. He often answers before we call."},{"sc":"ashleydown","n":"🏛️ Start small and faithful. God can do far more than we ask or think."},{"sc":"study","n":"😊 Feed your soul first each morning with God's word."}],"stops":{"gm4s1":{"story":[{"sc":"orphandining","n":"More orphan houses opened on Wilson Street: one for younger children, one for boys. Soon George was caring for well over a hundred children."},{"sc":"orphandining","n":"From 1838, there were many hard years. Often there was enough money only for the next meal. Sometimes not even that."},{"sc":"home","w":"marygroves","e":"worried","t":"George, there's nothing left for tomorrow's dinner. Not a penny."},{"sc":"home","w":"mueller","e":"smile","t":"Then let's pray. The Lord knows. He has never failed us yet."},{"sc":"home","n":"Again and again, help came just in time: a coin dropped in the box, a letter with money, a gift of food from someone who knew nothing of their need."},{"sc":"study","n":"George wrote it all in his journal. Sometimes help came an hour before a meal. Sometimes minutes before."},{"sc":"study","n":"George later wrote that, through all those years, the children never once had to go without a meal. God was never early, but he was never late."}],"quote":{"text":"Give us this day our daily bread.","who":"Matthew 6:11","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Enough for today","text":"God didn't give George years of money in advance. He gave him what he needed each day. That kept George close to God. Trust God for today's bread, and tomorrow's will come tomorrow."},"think":"Why do you think God often provided for George just in time, rather than far ahead?","quiz":[{"q":"How many children was George caring for soon after starting?","o":["Well over a hundred","Five","Ten thousand at once","None"],"why":"More houses opened on Wilson Street."},{"q":"What were things like from 1838?","o":["Hard years, often with only enough for the next meal","Easy and rich","No children came","The houses closed"],"why":"Sometimes not even that."},{"q":"What did George do when there was nothing left?","o":["Prayed","Borrowed money","Sent the children away","Asked the newspaper"],"why":"He kept his rules."},{"q":"How did help usually come?","o":["Just in time","Years early","Never","Always too late"],"why":"Sometimes minutes before a meal."},{"q":"Did the children ever miss a meal?","o":["George wrote that they never did","Every week","Often","Every day"],"why":"God was never late."}]},"gm4s2":{"story":[{"sc":"orphandining","n":"People who knew the orphan houses told one story again and again. Here it is."},{"sc":"orphandining","n":"One morning, the children came in for breakfast and sat down at the long tables. But the plates were empty. There was no food in the house, and no money to buy any."},{"sc":"orphandining","w":"orphangirl","e":"worried","t":"Mr Müller, there's nothing to eat!"},{"sc":"orphandining","w":"mueller","e":"smile","t":"Children, let's thank God for what he's going to give us to eat."},{"sc":"orphandining","n":"And he prayed, thanking God for breakfast, though there was none."},{"sc":"orphandining","n":"Just as he finished, there was a knock at the door. It was the baker."},{"sc":"orphandining","w":"baker","e":"worried","t":"Mr Müller, I couldn't sleep last night. I felt God wanted me to bake bread for your children. So I got up at two o'clock and baked it. Here it is!"},{"sc":"orphandining","n":"A moment later, there was another knock. The milkman's cart had broken down right outside the orphan house."},{"sc":"orphandining","w":"milkman","e":"big","t":"My cart's broken and the milk will go sour before I can fix it. Would the children like it? Free!"},{"sc":"orphandining","n":"That morning, the children had fresh bread and milk for breakfast, because their Father in heaven had already answered before they asked."}],"quote":{"text":"And it shall come to pass, that before they call, I will answer; and while they are yet speaking, I will hear.","who":"Isaiah 65:24","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Thank him in advance","text":"George thanked God for breakfast before there was any. That's faith: trusting God's character before you see the answer. God had already moved the baker in the night, before anyone prayed."},"think":"Why do you think George thanked God for breakfast before it arrived? What's something you could thank God for in advance?","quiz":[{"q":"What was on the plates when the children sat down?","o":["Nothing","Bread","Rice","Cake"],"why":"No food in the house."},{"q":"What did George do?","o":["Thanked God for the breakfast to come","Sent the children back to bed","Went shopping","Cried"],"why":"Faith in advance."},{"q":"Why had the baker baked bread?","o":["He felt God wanted him to, in the night","He was paid","It was his birthday","By mistake"],"why":"He got up at two o'clock."},{"q":"What happened to the milkman?","o":["His cart broke down outside","He got lost","He was late","He sold all his milk"],"why":"He gave the milk away."},{"q":"What does Isaiah 65:24 say?","o":["Before they call, I will answer","I will never answer","Call louder","Wait for ever"],"why":"God answered before they asked."}]},"gm4s3":{"story":[{"sc":"bristol","n":"By the 1840s, the Wilson Street houses were bursting with children. And the neighbours were unhappy."},{"sc":"bristol","w":"neighbour","e":"worried","t":"Mr Müller, with the greatest respect, the noise of so many children is very hard on us. And the drains can't cope."},{"sc":"bristol","n":"George didn't argue. He thought they had a point. Instead, he prayed. Should he build a proper orphan house of his own, out of the crowded streets?"},{"sc":"study","n":"He prayed for weeks. Then he decided: yes. He would build a big house for three hundred children, on open land with fresh air."},{"sc":"ashleydown","n":"He found land on a hill called {Ashley Down}. Money came in, without him asking. In 1849, the first great orphan house opened, with room for three hundred children."},{"sc":"ashleydown","n":"And George kept praying, bigger and bigger. Over the next twenty years he built five huge stone houses on Ashley Down, with room for more than two thousand children at once."},{"sc":"ashleydown","w":"mueller","e":"big","t":"Every brick of these houses was paid for by God, in answer to prayer. Not one penny was asked from anyone."},{"sc":"ashleydown","n":"The buildings still stand in Bristol today, more than 170 years later."}],"quote":{"text":"Now unto him that is able to do exceeding abundantly above all that we ask or think.","who":"Ephesians 3:20","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Bigger than we think","text":"George started with one rented house. God ended up providing five huge buildings for over two thousand children. God can do far more than we ask or imagine. Start with faithful steps, and let him grow it."},"think":"George listened kindly to his neighbours' complaint, and it led to something much bigger. How can criticism sometimes lead to good things?","quiz":[{"q":"Why were the Wilson Street neighbours unhappy?","o":["The noise and the drains","The children stole","The houses were ugly","They weren't"],"why":"So many children!"},{"q":"How did George respond to the complaint?","o":["He didn't argue, but prayed about building","He shouted","He ignored them","He moved to London"],"why":"He thought they had a point."},{"q":"When did the first Ashley Down house open?","o":["1849","1836","1898","1805"],"why":"For three hundred children."},{"q":"How many houses did George build on Ashley Down?","o":["Five","One","Fifty","Two"],"why":"Room for over two thousand children."},{"q":"What does Ephesians 3:20 say God can do?","o":["Exceeding abundantly above all we ask or think","Only a little","Exactly what we ask","Nothing"],"why":"Bigger than we think."}]},"gm4s4":{"story":[{"sc":"study","n":"With so many children to care for, how did George keep going? In 1841, he discovered a secret that changed his life."},{"sc":"study","n":"He realised that he had been rushing into prayer and work each morning, often feeling dry and distracted."},{"sc":"study","w":"mueller","e":"smile","t":"The first and most important thing I must do each day is not to serve the Lord, but to get my soul happy in the Lord."},{"sc":"study","n":"So every morning, before anything else, he opened his Bible and read slowly. He would {meditate} on a verse, thinking about it, until it turned naturally into prayer and thanks."},{"sc":"study","n":"It changed everything. His heart was full of joy and strength before the day's problems began."},{"sc":"study","n":"George read the whole Bible through about two hundred times in his life. Not to tick a box, but because it fed his soul like bread."},{"sc":"orphandining","n":"Two thousand children needed feeding every day. But George knew that his own soul needed feeding first."}],"quote":{"text":"I saw more clearly than ever, that the first great and primary business to which I ought to attend every day was, to have my soul happy in the Lord.","who":"George Müller, 1841","tag":"Müller's words"},"lesson":{"title":"Feed your soul first","text":"George's secret wasn't working harder. It was starting each day with God's word until his heart was happy in the Lord. Before you check your phone each morning, try reading one verse slowly and talking to God about it."},"think":"What is usually the first thing you do in the morning? What difference might it make to start with God's word?","quiz":[{"q":"What did George discover in 1841?","o":["The first business of each day is to get his soul happy in the Lord","A new building","Gold","A new language"],"why":"His life-changing secret."},{"q":"What was going wrong before?","o":["He rushed into prayer and work feeling dry","He slept too late","He didn't pray","He was ill"],"why":"Distracted and dry."},{"q":"What did George do first each morning?","o":["Read the Bible slowly and meditate on it","Count the money","Check the kitchen","Go for a walk"],"why":"Until it turned into prayer."},{"q":"About how many times did George read through the Bible?","o":["About two hundred","Once","Ten","Never"],"why":"It fed his soul."},{"q":"What does Psalm 1:2 say the blessed person does?","o":["Meditates on God's law day and night","Sleeps all day","Works all night","Forgets God's law"],"why":"Delight in God's word."}]}}},"gm5":{"challenge":[{"q":"What did children learn at Ashley Down?","o":["Reading, writing, sums, singing and the Bible","Nothing","Only cooking","Only fighting"],"why":"A real education."},{"q":"What did boys get when they left?","o":["An apprenticeship to learn a trade","A sword","A ship","A farm"],"why":"A future."},{"q":"How many children did George's homes care for in his lifetime?","o":["More than ten thousand","Ten","A hundred","A million"],"why":"In Bristol."},{"q":"How long were George and Mary married?","o":["About forty years","One year","Ten years","Sixty years"],"why":"Partners in faith."},{"q":"What did George preach at Mary's funeral?","o":["Thou art good, and doest good","Life is unfair","Never marry","Nothing"],"why":"Psalm 119:68."},{"q":"Who travelled the world with George?","o":["Susannah","Henry Craik","Beta","Mary Slessor"],"why":"For seventeen years."},{"q":"About how many miles did they travel?","o":["About two hundred thousand","About two hundred","About two million","About twenty"],"why":"Like going around the world eight times."},{"q":"In what year did George die?","o":["1898","1805","1870","1915"],"why":"Aged ninety-two."},{"q":"How much money did George receive for God's work without asking?","o":["About one and a half million pounds","Ten pounds","Nothing","A billion pounds"],"why":"A huge fortune then."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Train up a ___ in the way he should go.\"","o":["child","soldier","dog","king"],"why":"Proverbs 22:6."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What is the one big lesson of George Müller's life?","o":["God is real and hears prayer","Money is everything","Never travel","Work alone"],"why":"He spent seventy years proving it."},"choices":[{"title":"Start a prayer journal","q":"You've just finished George Müller's story. Your friend asks, \"So what will you actually do differently?\"","o":[{"t":"Start a prayer journal: write down needs, pray, and record answers.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! That's exactly what George did for seventy years."},{"t":"Nothing. It's just a story.","f":"A true story is an invitation."},{"t":"Wait until you're old like George.","f":"George started trusting God as a young man."}]},{"title":"When something sad happens","q":"Your pet dies and you feel angry with God. Your mum gently asks what you're feeling.","o":[{"t":"Tell her honestly, cry if you need to, and remember that God is still good.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! George wept, and still said, 'Thou art good.'"},{"t":"Decide God doesn't care.","f":"Grief is real, but God's goodness is too."},{"t":"Pretend you're fine.","f":"Faith doesn't mean hiding tears."}]},{"title":"Grandma's energy","q":"Your grandmother, who is seventy-five, wants to start teaching a Bible club for children in her street. Your cousins laugh at the idea.","o":[{"t":"Encourage her and offer to help.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! George started a world tour at seventy."},{"t":"Laugh too. She's too old.","f":"They shall still bring forth fruit in old age."},{"t":"Tell her to rest instead.","f":"Rest is good, but so is serving."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"ashleydown","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's how you'll use George Müller's story for the rest of your life."},{"sc":"ashleydown","n":"🎓 Care about people's whole lives, not just today's needs."},{"sc":"church","n":"💐 In sorrow, hold on to this: God is good, and does good."},{"sc":"seashore","n":"🌍 You're never too young to start, or too old to keep going."},{"sc":"study","n":"📒 Keep a prayer journal. Ask God, trust God, and thank God."},{"sc":"throneglory","n":"🍞 Well done! You've finished George Müller's story."}],"stops":{"gm5s1":{"story":[{"sc":"ashleydown","n":"Life at Ashley Down was very different from the streets or the workhouse."},{"sc":"orphandining","n":"The children had clean clothes, warm beds and three meals a day. They went to school and learned to read, write, sum, sing and know the Bible."},{"sc":"orphandining","w":"orphangirl","e":"big","t":"We get porridge for breakfast, and on Sundays there's even pudding!"},{"sc":"ashleydown","n":"When they were old enough, boys were found an {apprenticeship} to learn a trade, and girls were trained for jobs too. Many became teachers, nurses and skilled workers."},{"sc":"ashleydown","n":"Every child leaving Ashley Down was given a Bible, new clothes, and George's prayers."},{"sc":"ashleydown","w":"oldorphan","e":"smile","t":"I came to Ashley Down with nothing. I left with a trade, a Bible and a Father in heaven. I'll never forget it."},{"sc":"ashleydown","n":"Over his lifetime, more than ten thousand children were cared for in George Müller's orphan houses."}],"quote":{"text":"Train up a child in the way he should go: and when he is old, he will not depart from it.","who":"Proverbs 22:6","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"More than survival","text":"George didn't just keep orphans alive. He gave them an education, a skill, faith and a future. Real care thinks about the whole person and their whole life."},"think":"Why do you think George made sure the children learned a trade, not just how to survive?","quiz":[{"q":"What did children get at Ashley Down?","o":["Clean clothes, warm beds, meals and school","Nothing","Only work","Only bread"],"why":"Very different from the workhouse."},{"q":"What did boys get when they were old enough?","o":["An apprenticeship to learn a trade","A horse","A ship","A house"],"why":"Girls were trained for jobs too."},{"q":"What was every leaving child given?","o":["A Bible, new clothes and prayers","A bag of gold","A train ticket only","Nothing"],"why":"A good start."},{"q":"How many children were cared for over George's lifetime?","o":["More than ten thousand","One hundred","Fifty","One million"],"why":"An amazing number."},{"q":"What does Proverbs 22:6 say?","o":["Train up a child in the way he should go","Children don't need training","Leave children alone","Only train boys"],"why":"Real care shapes a whole life."}]},"gm5s2":{"story":[{"sc":"home","n":"In February 1870, George's beloved wife Mary became very ill with fever. After forty years together, she died."},{"sc":"home","n":"George was heartbroken. She had been his partner in every prayer and every struggle."},{"sc":"church","n":"At her funeral, George stood up to preach. Everyone wondered what he would say."},{"sc":"church","w":"oldmueller","e":"worried","t":"My text today is Psalm 119, verse 68: 'Thou art good, and doest good.'"},{"sc":"church","n":"Even in the deepest sorrow of his life, George declared that God is good and does good. He didn't pretend not to be sad. He wept. But he still trusted God's goodness."},{"sc":"home","n":"The next year, George married Susannah Sanger, a kind Christian friend. She would travel the world with him."},{"sc":"home","w":"susannah","e":"smile","t":"George, let's go wherever God sends us, together."}],"quote":{"text":"Thou art good, and doest good; teach me thy statutes.","who":"Psalm 119:68","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"God is good, even in grief","text":"When George lost Mary, he preached that God is good and does good. Faith doesn't mean not crying. It means holding on to God's goodness even through tears."},"think":"How can someone believe God is good even when something very sad happens?","quiz":[{"q":"When did Mary Müller die?","o":["February 1870","1830","1898","1849"],"why":"After forty years together."},{"q":"What verse did George preach on at her funeral?","o":["Psalm 119:68, 'Thou art good, and doest good'","John 11:35","Genesis 1:1","Psalm 23:1"],"why":"Even in sorrow."},{"q":"Did George pretend not to be sad?","o":["No, he wept but still trusted God","Yes, he smiled all day","He didn't care","He hid away"],"why":"Faith with tears."},{"q":"Who did George marry the next year?","o":["Susannah Sanger","Mary Slessor","Hannah","Asano"],"why":"She travelled the world with him."},{"q":"What does Psalm 119:68 say about God?","o":["Thou art good, and doest good","God is distant","God is angry","God is sleeping"],"why":"Even in grief."}]},"gm5s3":{"story":[{"sc":"ashleydown","n":"When George was seventy, most people his age were resting. George had a different idea."},{"sc":"ashleydown","w":"oldmueller","e":"big","t":"Others can run the orphan houses now. God has given me strength. I want to tell the world what he has done."},{"sc":"seashore","n":"From 1875, George and Susannah travelled for seventeen years, to forty-two countries across Europe, America, Asia and Australia."},{"sc":"seashore","n":"They sailed on steamships, rode trains and travelled about two hundred thousand miles. That's like going around the world eight times!"},{"sc":"church","n":"Everywhere he went, George told the same simple message: God is real. God hears prayer. Trust him."},{"sc":"church","n":"It's thought that around three million people heard him preach on those journeys."},{"sc":"church","w":"susannah","e":"smile","t":"George, you're over eighty and still preaching three times a week!"},{"sc":"church","w":"oldmueller","e":"smile","t":"As long as God gives me breath, I'll tell people about his faithfulness."}],"quote":{"text":"They shall still bring forth fruit in old age; they shall be fat and flourishing.","who":"Psalm 92:14","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Never too old","text":"At seventy, George started a seventeen-year world tour. You're never too young to start serving God, and never too old to keep going."},"think":"Who is an older person you know who is still serving God with energy? What can you learn from them?","quiz":[{"q":"How old was George when he began travelling the world?","o":["Seventy","Twenty","Forty","Ninety"],"why":"In 1875."},{"q":"For how many years did he travel?","o":["Seventeen","Two","Fifty","One"],"why":"With Susannah."},{"q":"How many countries did he visit?","o":["Forty-two","Two","Two hundred","Ten"],"why":"Across many continents."},{"q":"What was George's simple message?","o":["God is real, God hears prayer, trust him","Give me money","Visit Bristol","Nothing"],"why":"The same everywhere."},{"q":"What does Psalm 92:14 say about old age?","o":["They shall still bring forth fruit","Old people are useless","Rest and do nothing","It's too late"],"why":"Never too old."}]},"gm5s4":{"story":[{"sc":"ashleydown","n":"On the evening of 9 March 1898, George, now ninety-two, led a prayer meeting at Ashley Down as usual. Then he went to bed."},{"sc":"night","n":"Early the next morning, he was found peacefully gone, beside his bed. He had gone home to the Father he had trusted for so long."},{"sc":"bristol","n":"At his funeral, tens of thousands of people lined the streets of Bristol. Shops closed. Flags flew at half-mast. Many of the mourners were grown-up orphans who had been raised at Ashley Down."},{"sc":"study","n":"Over his lifetime, George received around one and a half million pounds for God's work, a huge fortune then. He never once asked anyone for money."},{"sc":"study","n":"He recorded tens of thousands of specific answers to prayer in his journals. When he died, he owned almost nothing."},{"sc":"ashleydown","n":"The work he began still continues in Bristol today, helping children and families, as a charity called the {Müller Trust}."},{"sc":"throneglory","n":"George Müller's life asks you a simple question: do you believe that God really hears your prayers? He spent seventy years proving that the answer is yes."}],"quote":{"text":"He being dead yet speaketh.","who":"Hebrews 11:4","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Your life, a testimony","text":"George's life still speaks: God is real, and he answers prayer. Your prayers, your faith and your answered prayers can become a testimony that speaks to others long after today."},"think":"Start a prayer journal this week, like George. What would you like to write in it first?","quiz":[{"q":"How old was George when he died?","o":["Ninety-two","Seventy","Sixty-six","Fifty"],"why":"In 1898."},{"q":"What did George do the evening before he died?","o":["Led a prayer meeting","Travelled abroad","Built a house","Wrote a novel"],"why":"As usual."},{"q":"Who lined the streets at his funeral?","o":["Tens of thousands of people, including grown-up orphans","Nobody","Only soldiers","Only his family"],"why":"Bristol honoured him."},{"q":"How much did George ever ask anyone for?","o":["Nothing","A million pounds","A penny a week","Ten pounds"],"why":"He told his needs only to God."},{"q":"Does George's work still continue?","o":["Yes, as the Müller Trust charity in Bristol","No, it ended","Only in Prussia","Only online"],"why":"His life still speaks."}]}}}});
